---
import SEOHead from '../../components/SEOHead.astro';
const title = "Binary Puzzle Strategy: How to Solve Takuzu";
const description = "Learn how to solve binary puzzles (Takuzu) step by step: the three rules, balance counting, run breaking, and strategy for hard 10x10 grids.";

const faqs = [
  { q: "Is Takuzu the same as a binary puzzle?", a: "Yes. Takuzu, Binary Puzzle, and Binairo are different names for the same 0 and 1 logic game: equal numbers per line, no three identical digits in a row, and no duplicate rows or columns in Takuzu mode. GridPaw uses the name Binary Puzzle on the page and the Takuzu rules under the hood." },
  { q: "Do binary puzzles have only one solution?", a: "On GridPaw, yes. Every level is generated and then verified by a solver to have exactly one solution before it reaches you. If your logical moves run out, a deduction you missed is hiding somewhere, not a second answer." },
  { q: "What sizes and difficulties does GridPaw offer?", a: "Easy is 6x6, Medium is 8x8, and Hard is 10x10. The hard tier opens with the fewest starting digits, about fifteen, while Easy and Medium start with roughly twenty filled cells." },
  { q: "How do GridPaw hints work?", a: "A hint reads the puzzle's unique solution and fills the cell you chose with its exact correct digit, either 0 or 1. There is no partial clue: a hint settles that cell completely." },
  { q: "Are binary puzzles hard to learn?", a: "Not at all. The three rules fit in a sentence, and the first techniques are just spotting pairs and counting. Most people finish an easy 6x6 within a few minutes of learning the rules." },
];
