    <h1>How to Solve Binary Puzzles: A Friendly Takuzu Guide</h1>
    <p style="text-align:center;color:#6f5651;margin-bottom:1rem;">Master Binary Puzzle (Takuzu) with step by step techniques for every grid size.</p>

    <section class="seo-content">
      <p>Binary puzzles go by many names: Takuzu, Binairo, or simply "the 0 and 1 grid." Whatever you call them, the appeal is the same: a tiny rule set that produces deep, satisfying deductions. In a binary puzzle every cell is either <strong>0</strong> or <strong>1</strong>, and three golden laws make the solve. This guide walks through the binary puzzle rules, then builds a step by step method you can apply from your first 6x6 game to a hard 10x10 and beyond. Prefer to learn by doing? <a href="/binary/">Play a binary puzzle on GridPaw</a> and keep this page open as your coach.</p>

      <div class="cta-box">New here? <a href="/binary/">Play free binary puzzle</a> · <a href="/binary/">Start with Easy 6x6</a></div>

      <h2>Binary Puzzle Rules: The Three Golden Laws</h2>
      <p>The whole game rests on three rules. The engine behind <a href="/binary/">GridPaw's takuzu</a> checks all three on every move, and once you internalize them you will recognize the same structure in any binary puzzle book, app, or newspaper.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Balance.</strong> Each row and each column must end with an equal number of 0s and 1s. On a 6x6 board that means three of each per line; on a 10x10 board, five of each. A line can never let one digit pass half its length, and no cells may be left over at the end.</li>
        <li><strong>No three in a row.</strong> You can never write three identical digits next to each other, horizontally or vertically. Runs of two are fine: 0 0 is legal, while 0 0 0 is not.</li>
        <li><strong>Unique lines.</strong> In Takuzu mode, which GridPaw uses by default, no two rows may be identical and no two columns may be identical. Every line has to carry a distinct pattern.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>That is the complete ruleset. Everything else in this guide is technique: how to turn those three laws into forced moves. If you enjoy puzzles built from a few clean rules, you will probably love our <a href="/akari/">Akari (Light Up) guide</a> too; it is the same spirit, with bulbs instead of digits.</p>

      <h2>How to Solve Takuzu Step by Step: Opening Moves</h2>
      <p>Every solve starts the same way: sweep the grid for cells the rules force immediately. Use this order and you will rarely stall.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Hit the doubles.</strong> Wherever two identical digits sit side by side, both outer neighbors must be the opposite digit. A row reading 1 1 _ forces the third cell to 0, and _ 0 0 forces the cell before the pair to 1. Scan rows and columns alike.</li>
        <li><strong>Fill the gap between equals.</strong> If two identical digits are separated by exactly one empty cell, like 0 _ 0, the middle cell must be the opposite digit. A 0 there would make 0 0 0, which breaks rule two, so it has to be 1. The same holds for 1 _ 1.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the walls.</strong> At the edge of the grid a pair flush against the border behaves the same way. In _ 1 1 at the start of a row, the first cell must be 0.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>These three opening moves come from the no-three rule alone. They are fast, safe, and they usually unlock a chain of further placements on easy boards. GridPaw's <a href="/binary/">free takuzu</a> makes the payoff visible: an easy 6x6 opens with about twenty given digits, so the first sweep tends to settle half of your row ends for you.</p>

