      <h2>Balance Technique: Count Zeros and Ones in Every Line</h2>
      <p>Once the no-three moves run dry, switch to counting. The balance law is a scanner, not a chooser: it tells you when a digit's quota is full.</p>
      <p>On a 6x6 board every row needs three 0s and three 1s. The moment a row already holds three of one digit, every remaining empty cell in that row must be the other digit. The same law runs down the columns. Watch lines that sit close to their quota, because they convert fastest.</p>
      <p>There is a subtler version of the same law, and GridPaw's checker uses it on every move. A line is only legal when its empty cells can exactly absorb the missing 0s and 1s. If a row has three blanks but only needs two more digits to reach its split, that row can never be completed, so whatever move created that state is dead. Use this to reject candidate cells before you even write them.</p>
      <p><strong>Whenever a line's remaining blanks exactly match its missing digits, every blank gets a fixed value.</strong> Suppose a row has three blanks, already contains two 0s and one 1, and still needs one more 0 and two more 1s. Those three blanks are now fully determined: one of them is 0 and the other two are 1. Combine this with the no-three rule and the 1 _ 1 gap rule to work out which blank is which.</p>

      <h2>No Three in a Row: Finding Forced Cells</h2>
      <p>Rule two is the most visual, which makes it the most fun to exploit. Scan every row and column for the two dangerous shapes.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Double, then blank:</strong> 0 0 _ or 1 1 _. The blank is forced to the opposite digit.</li>
        <li><strong>Blank, then double:</strong> _ 0 0 or _ 1 1. The blank ahead of the pair takes the opposite digit.</li>
        <li><strong>Equals with a gap:</strong> 0 _ 0 and 1 _ 1. The middle cell flips to the other digit.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Notice what is <em>not</em> on that list: 0 _ 1. Both values can complete it, 0 0 1 or 0 1 1, so it is neutral. Do not waste time on shapes that are not forced.</p>
      <p>A fast way to find these is to re-check the neighbors of every pair you already placed. Each new digit is a potential member of a fresh double or a fresh gap, so after every placement, scan its two row neighbors and its two column neighbors. GridPaw flags mistakes live: place a digit that creates a third in a line and the game calls the three-in-a-row error instantly, which makes practicing the scan completely safe.</p>

      <h2>Row and Column Uniqueness: No Two Lines Alike</h2>
      <p>Rule three is the one most beginners forget, and it is the key to finishing boards. In Takuzu mode every row must be a pattern no other row repeats, and every column must do the same.</p>
      <p>The practical payoff shows up late in a solve. When a board is down to its last unfilled row, that row can only take one of the balanced patterns that is not already sitting in the grid. List the completed rows, see which valid pattern is missing, and you have the row's full contents; the column givens and the no-three rule then pin down the exact order.</p>
      <p>Earlier in the solve, use the copy shortcut as a constraint. Once a row is complete, compare other rows against its prefix. If a nearly finished row already shares several digits with a complete row, the uniqueness rule can force or forbid specific cells to keep them apart. Columns behave identically. If this single-solution discipline feels familiar, it should: <a href="/hashi/">Hashi (Bridges)</a> players live by the same logic, and our Hashi guide shows how uniqueness thinking scales once you add bridge counts.</p>

      <h2>Hard Takuzu Strategy: Mastering the 10x10 Board</h2>
      <p>Hard takuzu is where the three rules start talking to each other. GridPaw's Hard tier is a 10x10 board that opens with only about fifteen givens, so early doubles are scarce and the first deduction often comes from balance rather than from pairs.</p>
      <p>The strategy that scales is <strong>candidate narrowing</strong>. For a stubborn cell, list what it cannot be: a digit that breaks a run, pushes a line past its quota, or would duplicate a row. If one digit survives, place it. When both survive, look one move ahead: try a digit, apply the three rules to the cells it touches, and see whether the board breaks. If it does, the other digit was forced. This is the same elimination habit you use in <a href="/akari/">Akari</a>, just adapted to 0 and 1.</p>
      <p>Do not be shy with hints. GridPaw's hint system reads the puzzle's unique solution and returns the exact correct value for the cell you picked, either 0 or 1, never a vague nudge.</p>
      <p>What about a 14x14 hard takuzu? That size is common in printed puzzle collections and larger mobile apps, and the rules do not change: seven 0s and seven 1s per line, no triples, no duplicate lines. The difference is that counting carries far more weight. Practicing on GridPaw's hard 10x10s is the best warmup for those giants, because the 10x10 already forces you to track quotas across a wide grid while the pair scans stay fast.</p>

      <div class="cta-box">Ready to test the technique? <a href="/binary/">Play takuzu free</a> · <a href="/binary/">Try a Medium 8x8</a></div>

      <h2>Binary Puzzle Tips for Beginners</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Start on 6x6.</strong> Easy boards give you about twenty digits and almost write themselves. Medium 8x8 and hard 10x10 can wait a few days.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep a running count.</strong> Track how many 0s and 1s each line has. The moment a line hits its quota, every blank in it is decided.</li>
        <li><strong>Scan after every move.</strong> Each digit you place creates new doubles and new gaps. Re-check its row and column neighbors before looking further.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not guess early.</strong> On a well-made puzzle the first twenty moves are usually all forced. If you stall, you missed a balance or a gap, not a miracle.</li>
        <li><strong>Treat hints as teachers.</strong> When stuck, ask the hint, note the value it proves, and reverse-engineer why that digit had to be there.</li>
        <li><strong>Play daily.</strong> Ten minutes of takuzu is a lovely warm habit, and the pattern library builds surprisingly fast.</li>
      </ul>

